/**

@page tutorials_exahype2_applications_exagrype_configure Configure ExaGRyPE

@image html tutorials/exahype2/exagrype/arty.png

ExaGRyPE is Peano's extension for numerical relativity and is built as part of
Peano. For general remarks on how to install Peano, please see
\ref page_installation_home. This page is designed to be self-contained,
i.e. it should include all the steps you need to run the example codes.


# Get the code

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
git clone https://github.com/tobiasweinzierl/peano.git
cd Peano
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~


# Prerequisites

Besides a C++20 compiler with OpenMP support (and MPI if you want to run
in parallel on a cluster), ExaGRyPE needs

- Python 3 for the code generation (see the Python environment below);
- the GNU Scientific Library (GSL), but only for the black hole scenarios:
  their TwoPunctures initial data relies on GSL. The gauge wave does not need
  it.

On a workstation, GSL is typically installed via the package manager, e.g.

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
sudo apt install libgsl-dev      # Debian/Ubuntu
sudo dnf install gsl-devel       # Fedora/RHEL
brew install gsl                 # macOS
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

On a cluster, load the corresponding module (e.g. \c module load \c gsl)
before you configure, and load the proper modules for your compiler and MPI
(see \ref tutorials_exahype2_applications_exagrype_clusters).


# Configure and build Peano

Configure and compile Peano in the Peano root directory. With GCC and
Autotools, a minimal setup reads

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
libtoolize; aclocal; autoconf; autoheader; cp src/config.h.in .; automake --add-missing
./configure --enable-exahype --enable-blockstructured --enable-loadbalancing --enable-particles \
            --enable-exagrype --with-gsl --with-multithreading=omp \
            CXXFLAGS="-std=c++20 -O3 -fopenmp -Wno-attributes" LDFLAGS="-fopenmp"
make -j4
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

Give make an explicit number of parallel jobs. A plain \c make \c -j starts
as many compiler processes as there are files to compile, which exhausts the
memory of a workstation.

On macOS, \c libtoolize is called \c glibtoolize, and Apple's clang does not
support \c -fopenmp. Install GCC via Homebrew and pass it to configure, e.g.
\c CC=gcc-16 \c CXX=g++-16 (use the version number of your GCC). Do not
configure with \c CPPFLAGS or \c LDFLAGS pointing to Homebrew's \c libomp:
Peano's libraries then pick up its \c omp.h, while the generated
applications use GCC's, and the executables crash at start-up.

On a cluster with the Intel toolchain and MPI, the call becomes

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
./configure CC=icx CXX=icpx --with-mpi=mpiicpx --with-multithreading=omp \
            CXXFLAGS="-Ofast -std=c++20 -qopenmp -march=native -mtune=native -fma -fomit-frame-pointer -Wno-attributes=clang:: -w" \
            LDFLAGS="-qopenmp" \
            --enable-exahype --enable-blockstructured --enable-loadbalancing --enable-particles \
            --enable-exagrype --with-gsl
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

The flags have the following roles:

- \c --enable-exahype, \c --enable-blockstructured and \c --enable-loadbalancing
  build ExaHyPE 2, on top of which ExaGRyPE sits.
- \c --enable-particles builds the particle toolbox, which ExaGRyPE uses for
  its tracers.
- \c --enable-exagrype builds the ExaGRyPE library, which hosts the CCZ4
  kernels and the initial conditions (see \ref page_exagrype_library). It
  requires ExaHyPE 2 and is built for 3d only. Without it, the generated
  ExaGRyPE Makefiles do not link.
- \c --with-gsl compiles the TwoPunctures black hole initial data into the
  ExaGRyPE library and adds GSL to the link line of all generated Makefiles.
  You can drop it if you only run the gauge wave. If you build a black hole
  scenario against a Peano configured without GSL, the build stops with an
  error message that asks you to reconfigure.

\c --with-gsl does not take a path. Configure asks \c pkg-config for GSL and
otherwise falls back to \c -lgsl \c -lgslcblas \c -lm. If GSL is installed
in a non-standard location that \c pkg-config does not know, add its
directories by hand:

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
./configure ... --with-gsl CXXFLAGS="... -I/path/to/gsl/include" LDFLAGS="... -L/path/to/gsl/lib"
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

If you prefer CMake, build out-of-tree with the equivalent options:

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
mkdir build && cd build
cmake .. -DENABLE_EXAHYPE=ON -DENABLE_BLOCKSTRUCTURED=ON -DENABLE_LOADBALANCING=ON -DENABLE_PARTICLES=ON \
         -DENABLE_EXAGRYPE=ON -DWITH_GSL=ON -DWITH_MULTITHREADING=omp
make -j4
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

CMake locates GSL via its \c FindGSL module. For a non-standard installation,
pass \c -DGSL_ROOT_DIR=/path/to/gsl.

Missing libraries may lead to configuration or compilation errors; please check
\ref tutorials_exahype2_applications_exagrype_faq if necessary. You only need to
compile Peano once as long as your working environment and the Peano kernel code
remain unchanged. We recommend re-compiling it after you pull a new code version
or change working environments (e.g. when you switch between different login
nodes of a cluster).

To clean the Peano root directory completely, e.g. before a fresh configure,
use the following lines:

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
rm -rf aclocal.m4 compile src/config.* configure *sh Makefile Makefile.in missing stamp* ar-lib autom4te.cache *.log libtool depcomp *.scan
find . -name Makefile -delete
find . -name Makefile.in -delete
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~


# Python environment

ExaGRyPE's code generation relies on several Python packages. The recommended
way to install them is an editable install from the Peano root directory (where
the \c pyproject.toml file resides). This installs the full set of required
dependencies and sets the Python path to the Peano modules automatically, so
there is no need to export \c PYTHONPATH by hand. For the complete dependency
list and alternative setups (e.g. setting \c PYTHONPATH manually), see
\ref page_installation_home.

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
python3 -m venv .venv
source .venv/bin/activate
pip install --upgrade pip
pip install -e .
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~


# Where to go next

ExaGRyPE currently offers two entry points:

- The new Python API \c exagrype.api layers on top of \c exahype2.api. It
  offers Finite Volumes and the fourth-order Finite Differences
  (\c exahype2.api.rkfd4, optionally with Kreiss-Oliger dissipation and
  lopsided advection) on regular meshes, the gauge wave, and a single black
  hole with TwoPunctures initial data. Binary black holes, adaptive mesh
  refinement for the Finite Differences and tracers are not available yet.
  Its tutorials are Jupyter notebooks in \c tutorials/exahype2/exagrype:
  - \ref tutorials_exahype2_exagrype_521_gauge-wave runs the gauge wave with
    Finite Volumes and studies their convergence;
  - \ref tutorials_exahype2_exagrype_522_single-black-hole evolves a single
    black hole with Finite Volumes;
  - \ref tutorials_exahype2_exagrype_523_higher-order-fd evolves the same
    black hole with fourth-order Finite Differences.

  \ref benchmarks_exagrype_gauge_wave, \ref benchmarks_exagrype_single_black_hole
  and \ref page_exagrype_library provide further details.
- \c applications/exahype2/ccz4 hosts the main script \c ccz4.py, which
  provides all scenarios (gauge wave, single and binary black holes), tracers
  and the different numerical schemes. The remaining ExaGRyPE tutorials work
  with this script:
  ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
  cd applications/exahype2/ccz4
  ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
  The script builds on the legacy Finite Differences
  \c exahype2.solvers.rkfd. They have been removed in favour of
  \c exahype2.api.rkfd4, so the script and the tutorials which use it do not
  run until they are ported to the new API.


